in 2017 14 year old molly russell took  her own life it was only after she died  that her parents discovered she'd spent  the last weeks of her life looking at  increasingly extreme images of self-harm  and suicide on social media we couldn't  understand why molly must have ended up  feeling  so hopeless so worthless because she'd  been such a  such a lovely lovely young person and  we're all looking forward to the  her future life  but finding that content  really explained to us how she must have  been affected by what she'd seen on  digital platforms  ian russell's campaigning was important  in shaping the new online safety bill  we now come to presentation of bill  secretary of state nadine doris after  three years in the making formally put  before parliament today online safety  bill no matter which platform you're  using tech companies will have a legal  duty to protect users from harmful  content the regulator ofcom will even  have powers to look inside the software  the algorithms that decide what content  gets pushed to which users  what we're saying is  deal with those harmful algorithms deal  with that online scam advertising don't  allow our children to have pornography  just popping up on their phones and  their screens don't use that allow those  algorithms to send our children to  suicide chat rooms protect our kids and  we'll also give journalists and  individuals robust protections for  freedom of speech  the bill has been welcomed by those  policing extremism too in the last year  we saw 20 children young people under 18  arrested in relation to terrorism  offences they're searching for answers  and finding the wrong ones in some  really dangerous online places  we approached all the big tech and  social media companies nobody wanted to  speak to us in a statement their trade  body tech uk said the bill applied to at  least 24 000 companies and they hoped  lawmakers would work with industry to  ensure it delivers its objectives but  online safety campaigners say there is a  lot of work to do  we were promised a systems and process  bill one that actually took a swipe  at the system of silicon valley and one  that would make our kids safe right  across the uk wherever they were  online and this bill does not do that  yet if the act coming out of parliament  rather than the bill going into  parliament does not protect the next  molly russell then it's not fit for  purpose and that is the marker for all  of us  the online safety bill aims to create  the world's toughest restrictions on  some tech companies that are richer and  more powerful than whole nations  lawmakers have their work cut out to  reduce online harm without limiting the  important and not so important freedoms  and benefits the online world brings us  all tom clark sky news
 

